@node Introduction
@unnumbered Introduction

@acronym{STARTER} (@emph{Simple distribuTed smART conTrollER}) is a
distributed automation core. Services expose typed @dfn{inputs} and
@dfn{outputs} over D-Bus and mDNS; the core applies near-natural-language
rules to those values.

@command{starter-configurable-service} (@acronym{STCS}) is a satellite
process that turns an @acronym{XML} configuration into a running bridge
between the outside world and those starter-core I/O objects. Instead of
writing a dedicated C service for every device or @acronym{API}, you
describe transports, parsers, datapoints, and bindings in a single file.

This manual is organised in two layers:

@itemize
@item
@emph{Presentation} chapters explain what the service is, how pieces fit
together, and how to build and run it---without drowning in every XML
attribute.
@item
The @ref{Configuration Reference} spells out every configuration option
for the shipped plugins. @ref{Writing Plugins} explains how to add your
own.
@end itemize

@node Concepts
@chapter Concepts

@node Pipeline Overview
@section Pipeline Overview

All live values inside @acronym{STCS} are @code{gdouble}. Text and binary
payloads are converted at the edges (readers and writers). The usual
directions are:

@example
external source
  → transport (read)
  → reader (parse → numbers)
  → datapoint (+ optional transforms)
  → bind → ST input
@end example

@example
ST output
  → bind → datapoint
  → writer (numbers → payload)
  → transport (write)
@end example

A second datapoint can be bound to the same @acronym{ST} output with
@code{role="feedback"} so that device state can be mirrored back and
manual overrides detected (@pxref{Command and Feedback}).

@node Component Roles
@section Component Roles

@table @asis
@item Transport
Moves opaque bytes between the service and the outside world (file,
@acronym{HTTP}, child process, @dots{}). A read transport delivers
payloads; a write transport accepts a payload and reports success or
failure.

@item Reader
Consumes payloads from exactly one read transport, extracts numbers, and
pushes them into datapoints.

@item Writer
Watches datapoints, builds a payload, and sends it through exactly one
write transport.

@item Datapoint
Named numeric value in the pipeline. Optional transforms sit on the
datapoint and reshape every push.

@item Transform
A pure numeric stage on a datapoint (formula, sampling window, @dots{}).

@item Binding
Connects a datapoint to a starter-core input or output declared in the
same configuration.
@end table

Each transport may be claimed by at most one reader @emph{or} one
writer---not both, and not shared.

@node Thinking in Pipelines
@section Thinking in Pipelines

@acronym{STCS} is not where you write automation logic. That belongs in
@command{starter-core} rules. This service is a @emph{bridge}: you shape
each external signal as a short pipeline that ends on an @acronym{ST}
input or starts from an @acronym{ST} output.

Ask three questions for every device or feed:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{What should enter STARTER?} A temperature, a clock, a switch
state---anything the rules might @emph{read}. That wants a
@emph{read pipeline}: transport → reader → datapoint →
@code{<bind @dots{} input="..."/>}.
@item
@strong{What should leave STARTER?} A setpoint, a relay command---anything
the rules might @emph{write}. That wants a @emph{write pipeline}:
@code{<bind @dots{} output="..."/>} → datapoint → writer → transport.
@item
@strong{Does the device report its own state?} If yes, add a
@emph{feedback} datapoint on the same output (@code{role="feedback"}) so
@acronym{STCS} can mirror the field and detect manual overrides
(@pxref{Command and Feedback}).
@end enumerate

Numeric cosmetics (unit conversion, rounding, averaging) stay on
datapoint transforms. Decisions such as ``turn the light on after
sunset'' stay in the core rules file.

@subheading Example: Time as an input

Suppose you want rules that depend on the time of day (for example
minutes since midnight). Time is not a lamp---it is a @emph{sensor} for
the automation brain.

@enumerate
@item
Choose a source: a small helper that prints JSON, an @acronym{HTTP}
clock @acronym{API}, or a file updated by cron.
@item
Configure a @emph{read} transport (@code{process-transport} or
@code{http-transport}) that fetches that payload on a schedule.
@item
Map the field with @code{json-reader} (or @code{stream-reader}) into a
datapoint, optionally through @code{text-to-value type="date"} to obtain
@code{day-minutes}.
@item
Bind the datapoint to an @acronym{ST} input, e.g.@: @code{CLK:DayMinutes}.
@end enumerate

From there, @command{starter-core} sees a normal input. A rule can compare
it to sunset, a schedule, or another service---without @acronym{STCS}
knowing anything about lamps.

@subheading Example: A light switched by those rules

The lamp is the @emph{actuator}. It usually needs both directions:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Command.} An @acronym{ST} output (e.g.@: @code{KitEastLight:switch})
bound to a command datapoint, a @code{stream-writer} that turns
@code{0}/@code{1} into @code{false}/@code{true}, and an
@code{http-transport} with @code{write-target=url} that calls the device
@acronym{RPC}.
@item
@strong{Feedback.} A second transport (or the same status endpoint on a
poll) read by @code{json-reader} into a feedback datapoint bound to the
@emph{same} output with @code{role="feedback"}.
@end itemize

The ``if time past sunset then light on'' logic lives in the core. This
service only keeps the output aligned with the physical switch and
pushes commands when the core (or a peer) changes the value.

@subheading Example: Sensor only

An outdoor temperature from a JSON @acronym{API} is a one-way story:
poll with @code{http-transport}, parse with @code{json-reader}, optional
@code{formula-transform} / @code{sampling-transform}, bind to an input.
No writer, no output.

@subheading Example: Setpoint only (with optional echo)

A boiler flow setpoint is primarily a write pipeline. If the cloud or
device also returns the current setpoint, add a feedback map so the
@acronym{ST} output does not drift from reality and so a change made on
the boiler panel can raise @code{MANUAL_OVERRIDE}.

@subheading A practical sketch

@example
Want: rules use time + drive a Shelly switch

STCS config:
  [read]  process/http ``now'' → json-reader → DayMinutes → INPUT
  [read]  http GetStatus     → json-reader → switch-fb   → OUTPUT (feedback)
  [write] OUTPUT (command)   → stream-writer → http Switch.Set

starter-core rules:
  when DayMinutes crosses threshold → set KitEastLight:switch
@end example

Draw the arrows on paper before editing @acronym{XML}. If a value is
consumed by rules, it must become an input. If a value is produced by
rules, it must become an output---and if the hardware talks back, give
that output a feedback path.

@node How Plugins Fit In
@section How Plugins Fit In

Almost every concrete behaviour (how to talk @acronym{HTTP}, how to parse
JSON, how to evaluate a formula) lives in a @dfn{plugin}: a shared library
(@code{.so}) loaded at runlevel 0.

@itemize
@item
At startup the service scans a plugin directory (see @option{--plugin-dir}
/ @env{STCS_PLUGIN_PATH}).
@item
Each module exports metadata (name, category, version). The name is what
you put in the config @code{plugin="..."} attribute.
@item
Categories are @code{transport}, @code{reader}, @code{writer}, and
@code{transform}.
@item
When the @acronym{XML} is parsed, @acronym{STCS} instantiates the named
plugin and feeds it its configuration subtree.
@end itemize

Shipped plugins cover common cases; custom devices or protocols are added
by dropping another @code{.so} in the plugin directory
(@pxref{Writing Plugins}).

@node Runlevels
@section Runlevels

The process boots through internal runlevels (advanced by a short timer):

@table @code
@item 0
Scan the plugin directory and register plugin types.
@item 1
Parse the @acronym{XML} config and instantiate objects.
@item 2
Wire bindings, reader sync hooks, and writer acknowledgement hooks.
@item 3
Start the starter-core server (service name / version from
@code{<global>}) so peer discovery and D-Bus I/O become available.
@end table

Feedback alignment before runlevel 3 is treated as startup sync (no manual
override). From runlevel 3 onward, idle mismatches can raise
@code{MANUAL_OVERRIDE} on the output (@pxref{Command and Feedback}).

@node Command and Feedback
@section Command and Feedback

@subheading Command path

@enumerate
@item
An @acronym{ST} output value changes (local set or peer write).
@item
The command binding pushes the value into the command datapoint
(transforms run).
@item
The writer rebuilds its template and starts a transport write.
@item
On completion or error the writer acknowledgement hook updates the output
write state (@code{idle} / @code{failed}). Pending writes skip
manual-override logic on feedback mismatches.
@end enumerate

Write transports keep a queue of depth 1: a newer write replaces a pending
one (the superseded attempt is cancelled).

@subheading Feedback path

@enumerate
@item
A reader begins/ends a parse; linked outputs are marked syncing.
@item
Mapped feedback datapoints receive device values.
@item
While syncing, the runtime can align the @acronym{ST} output to feedback.
@item
After runlevel 3, an @emph{idle} mismatch (device ≠ commanded, no pending
write, settle-delay elapsed) sets @code{MANUAL_OVERRIDE} and updates the
output from feedback.
@item
Optional @code{<override-timeout>} on the output clears the override after
a duration. @code{0} or absent means never auto-clear.
@end enumerate

Failed writes that later see a feedback mismatch align to the device and
clear the failed state without raising override.

Detailed bind attributes are in @ref{Bindings Reference}.

@node Building and Installing
@chapter Building and Installing

@node Dependencies
@section Dependencies

@itemize
@item GLib 2.0 / GObject / GIO / GModule
@item @command{starter-core} (pkg-config module @code{starter-core})
@item libxml2
@item json-glib
@item libsoup 3.x, or libsoup 2.4 @math{\ge} 2.58
@item Autotools (@command{autoconf}, @command{automake}, @command{libtool})
@item Texinfo (@command{makeinfo}) to build this manual
@end itemize

@node Build Steps
@section Build Steps

@example
$ ./autogen.sh
$ ./configure
$ make
$ sudo make install
@end example

Plugins install under the package plugin directory (override at runtime
with @option{--plugin-dir} or @env{STCS_PLUGIN_PATH}).

@example
$ make doc          # PDF manual
$ make -C doc info  # Info manual
@end example

@node Running the Service
@chapter Running the Service

@node Command Line
@section Command Line

@example
starter-configurable-service [OPTION...]
@end example

@table @option
@item -h, --help
Print a short usage summary (provided automatically by GLib
@code{GOptionContext}) and exit.

@item -c, --config=@var{FILE}
Path to the service configuration file (required for a useful run).

@item -P, --config-preprocessor=@var{CMD}
Shell command used to preprocess the config before parsing.
@code{@{F@}} is replaced with the config path. An empty value disables
preprocessing. Default may also come from
@env{STCS_CONFIG_PREPROCESSOR}.

@item -p, --plugin-dir=@var{DIR}
Directory to scan for @code{.so} plugins. Falls back to
@env{STCS_PLUGIN_PATH}, then the compile-time default install path.

@item -V, --version
Print the version string and exit.
@end table

@node Logging
@section Logging

The service uses GLib message logging. Domains follow component names
(e.g.@: @code{http-transport}, @code{datapoint}, @code{process-transport}).

@strong{DEBUG} and @strong{INFO} are off by default so journald is not
flooded on every poll. Enable them with @env{G_MESSAGES_DEBUG}:

@example
# everything
G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=all starter-configurable-service --config …

# one or more domains (space-separated)
G_MESSAGES_DEBUG="http-transport datapoint" \
  starter-configurable-service --config …
@end example

Under systemd, set it in the unit:

@example
[Service]
Environment=G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=all
@end example

@strong{WARNING}, @strong{CRITICAL}, and @strong{ERROR} always appear.

@node Configuration Overview
@chapter Configuration Overview

Root element:

@example
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<stcs version="1">
  <global>...</global>
  <transports>...</transports>
  <datapoints>...</datapoints>
  <readers>...</readers>
  <writers>...</writers>
  <inputs>...</inputs>
  <outputs>...</outputs>
  <bindings>...</bindings>
</stcs>
@end example

Top-level sections are conventional; include only what the service needs.
Element names and attributes for every shipped plugin are listed in
@ref{Configuration Reference}. Worked examples live under
@file{examples/space-track/}, @file{examples/shelly-ht/}, and
@file{examples/current-time/}.

@section Shipped Plugins

@table @code
@item file-transport
Read or write a local file.
@item http-transport
@acronym{HTTP} client (GET poll, POST/PUT body write, or URL write).
@item process-transport
Oneshot external command (stdout read or stdin write).
@item json-reader / stream-reader
Parse JSON or opaque byte streams into datapoints.
@item json-writer / stream-writer
Build JSON or text/binary templates from datapoints.
@item formula-transform
Evaluate a TinyExpr formula on each push.
@item sampling-transform
Reduce a sliding or held window of samples.
@end table

@node Configuration Reference
@chapter Configuration Reference

This chapter is the detailed option list for configuration authors.

@node Document and Global
@section Document and Global

@table @code
@item <stcs version="1">
Root element. @code{version} is the config schema version.
@item <service-name>
Human-readable identity registered with starter-core / mDNS.
@item <service-version>
@code{major.minor.micro}, each component in @math{0..255}.
@end table

@node Cron Reference
@section Cron Expressions

Where a transport accepts @code{<poll-cron>}, the schedule uses
@strong{6 or 7} fields:

@example
ms sec min hour dom month [dow]
@end example

Examples: @code{0 0 * * * *} (every minute on the clock);
@code{0 0 */15 * * *} (every 15 minutes); @code{0 */30 * * * *} (every
30 seconds).

@node Transports Reference
@section Transports

Declare under @code{<transports>}. Each entry needs @code{id} and
@code{plugin}.

@node File Transport Reference
@subsection file-transport

@example
<transport id="file-in" plugin="file-transport">
  <path>/var/tmp/in.json</path>
  <mode>read</mode>              <!-- read | write | append -->
  <read-mode>stream</read-mode>  <!-- stream | window -->
  <reopen>reopen</reopen>        <!-- keep | reopen -->
  <poll-cron>0 0 * * * *</poll-cron>
</transport>
@end example

@table @code
@item path
File path.
@item mode
@code{read}, @code{write}, or @code{append} (exclusive).
@item read-mode / write-mode
@code{stream} (whole file / stream) or @code{window} (fixed
@code{offset}+@code{size}).
@item reopen
@code{keep} or @code{reopen}: how the file descriptor is handled across
operations.
@item poll-cron
Optional for read: periodic re-read.
@item offset / size
Required for @code{window} mode (byte offset and length).
@end table

@node HTTP Transport Reference
@subsection http-transport

@itemize
@item @code{GET} with @code{<poll-cron>} = read.
@item @code{POST}/@code{PUT} (no poll) = write; payload as body by default.
@item @code{GET}/@code{POST}/@code{PUT} with
@code{<write-target>url</write-target>} = write by substituting
@code{@{@{payload@}@}} in @code{<url>} (percent-encoded); empty body.
@end itemize

@example
<transport id="http-status" plugin="http-transport">
  <url>https://example.com/api/v1/status</url>
  <method>GET</method>
  <headers>
    <header name="Accept" value="application/json"/>
  </headers>
  <poll-cron>0 0 */5 * * *</poll-cron>
  <cookie-jar>/var/tmp/cookies.txt</cookie-jar>
  <auth type="none"/>
</transport>
@end example

@table @code
@item url
Request URL. For URL writes, include @code{@{@{payload@}@}}.
@item method
@code{GET}, @code{POST}, or @code{PUT}.
@item write-target
@code{body} (default) or @code{url}.
@item headers / header
Optional request headers (@code{name} + @code{value}).
@item poll-cron
Required for read mode.
@item cookie-jar
Filesystem path for a persistent cookie jar. Omit for an in-memory jar
private to that transport. When two or more HTTP transports use the
@emph{same} path, they share the on-disk cookie store (for example login
and data transports reusing a session).
@item auth
See below.
@end table

Authentication (@code{<auth type="...">}):

@table @code
@item none
No credentials.
@item basic
@code{<username>}, @code{<password>}.
@item header
@code{<token>} sent as Authorization (Bearer-style).
@item form
Login to @code{<login-url>} with @code{<login-method>}, then
username/password or explicit @code{<body>} /
@code{<content-type>}. Optional @code{<relogin-on-status>} (e.g.@: 401).
@end table

@node Process Transport Reference
@subsection process-transport

Oneshot @code{exec} (no shell).

Mandatory: @code{<command>}, @code{<user>}, @code{<timeout-ms>} (@math{> 0}).

@example
<transport id="helper" plugin="process-transport">
  <command>/usr/local/bin/fetch-status</command>
  <arg>--json</arg>
  <user>nobody</user>
  <timeout-ms>5000</timeout-ms>
  <mode>read</mode>
  <poll-cron>0 0 * * * *</poll-cron>
</transport>
@end example

@table @code
@item command
Executable path (no shell).
@item arg
Repeatable argv element.
@item user
After @code{fork}, the child drops privileges to this account.
@item timeout-ms
On expiry the child is killed; the transport reports an error.
@item mode
@code{read} (default): capture stdout until EOF, wait for exit.
@code{write}: write the payload to stdin, close stdin, wait for exit.
@item workdir
Optional working directory.
@item poll-cron
Optional for read.
@end table

Stderr is logged, never mixed into the data payload. Non-zero exit status
is an error.

@node Datapoints Reference
@section Datapoints and Transforms

@example
<datapoint id="temp-rounded">
  <transform plugin="formula-transform">
    <expr>floor(# * 10 + 0.5) / 10</expr>
  </transform>
</datapoint>
@end example

Transforms are optional and applied in document order on every push.

@node Formula Transform Reference
@subsection formula-transform

Uses the embedded @uref{https://codeplea.com/tinyexpr,TinyExpr} library
(Lewis Van Winkle): arithmetic, comparisons, and the usual math helpers
(@code{floor}, @code{abs}, @code{sin}, @dots{}).

@table @code
@item expr
Expression string. @code{#} is rewritten to @code{VALUE} (the incoming
datapoint value) before compilation. The compiled expression is cached.
@end table

License and attribution: @ref{Third-party Components}, @file{COPYING.ZLIB}.

@node Sampling Transform Reference
@subsection sampling-transform

@example
<transform plugin="sampling-transform">
  <window samples="10"/>   <!-- or duration="5m" -->
  <reduce>avg</reduce>     <!-- avg | min | max | median -->
  <mode>sliding</mode>     <!-- sliding | hold -->
</transform>
@end example

@table @code
@item window samples="N"
Fixed-length sample window.
@item window duration="..."
Time window: @code{300}, @code{300s}, @code{5m}, @code{1h}.
Requires @code{mode=hold}.
@item reduce
@code{avg}, @code{min}, @code{max}, or @code{median}.
@item mode
@code{sliding} (default) or @code{hold}.
@item mode=sliding
Reduce over a sliding window on every push; when full, drop the oldest
sample.
@item mode=hold + samples
Reduce every @var{N} samples; hold the last reduced value between updates.
@item mode=hold + duration
Clock-aligned buckets. Emit when the first sample of a new bucket arrives.
Failed reads do not shift the phase.
@end table

@node Readers Reference
@section Readers

Readers bind with @code{transport="..."}.

@node JSON Reader Reference
@subsection json-reader

@example
<reader id="api" plugin="json-reader" transport="http-status">
  <map datapoint="temp-raw" path="$.temperature"/>
  <map datapoint="mode-fb" path="$.relay">
    <text-to-value type="map">
      <item text="off" value="0"/>
      <item text="on" value="1"/>
      <default value="0"/>
    </text-to-value>
  </map>
</reader>
@end example

@itemize
@item Same path may fan out to multiple datapoints.
@item Numeric and boolean JSON convert without @code{<text-to-value>}.
@item Missing paths log a warning and continue.
@item At most one @code{<text-to-value>} per map
(@pxref{Text Conversions Reference}).
@end itemize

@node Stream Reader Reference
@subsection stream-reader

Parses opaque @code{GBytes}. Each @code{<map>} needs @code{id=} and
@code{datapoint=}, plus either @code{match=} (regex) or
@code{offset=}+@code{length=} (bytes).

@table @code
@item group
Regex capture index (default 0 = whole match).
@item on-missing
@code{warning} (default) or @code{error}.
@item encoding
For byte maps: @code{ascii} (default), @code{uint8}, @code{int8},
@code{uint16be}/@code{le}, @code{int16be}/@code{le},
@code{uint32be}/@code{le}, @code{int32be}/@code{le}.
@item text-to-value
Optional; at most one per map. Allowed only on @emph{regex} maps, or on
byte maps with @code{encoding="ascii"}. Not valid with numeric byte
encodings (@code{uint8}, @code{int16be}, @dots{}). Same child elements as
json-reader (@pxref{Text Conversions Reference}).
@end table

@node Writers Reference
@section Writers

Writers bind with @code{transport="..."}. No flush at configure time; the
first write happens on the first datapoint change.

@table @code
@item write-attempts
Total attempts for the same bytes on transport failure (default 1,
minimum 1). Cancelled superseding writes are not retried.
@end table

@node JSON Writer Reference
@subsection json-writer

@example
<writer id="out-json" plugin="json-writer" transport="file-out">
  <write-attempts>3</write-attempts>
  <template><![CDATA[
@{ "setpoint": 0, "mode": "auto" @}
]]></template>
  <map datapoint="out-setpoint" path="$.setpoint"/>
  <map datapoint="out-mode" path="$.mode"/>
</writer>
@end example

@code{<template>} must be valid JSON. Maps update simple @code{$.a.b}
paths (no filters or array indices). The same path may be driven by
several datapoints: each @code{value-changed} writes that path (last
writer wins for overlapping updates). Optional @code{<value-to-text>}
per map (at most one).

@node Stream Writer Reference
@subsection stream-writer

@strong{Text} (@code{encoding="text"}, default): template with
@code{@{@{id@}@}} placeholders; maps are @code{id=}+@code{datapoint=} only.
Placeholders that are never updated stay as literal text in the output.

@strong{Binary} (@code{encoding="hex"}): hex template (whitespace
allowed); maps require @code{offset=}+@code{length=} and a numeric
@code{encoding} as in stream-reader. @code{value-to-text} is only valid
with @code{encoding="ascii"}.

@node Text Conversions Reference
@section Text Conversions

@subheading text-to-value (readers)

@table @code
@item type="date"
@code{<format>} (strftime-like), optional @code{<timezone>} (of the
string; empty/@code{UTC} default), optional @code{<local-timezone>} (for
day-relative kinds). @code{<value>}: @code{unix}, @code{unix-ms},
@code{day-minutes}, @code{day-seconds}, @code{day-ms}.
@item type="map"
@code{<item text="..." value="..."/>} plus optional
@code{<default value="..."/>}.
@end table

@subheading value-to-text (writers)

@table @code
@item type="date"
Format a numeric timestamp/day-offset (same child elements as above).
@item type="map"
@code{<item value="..." text="..."/>}, @code{<default text="..."/>}.
@end table

@node IO Reference
@section Inputs, Outputs, and Bindings

@node Inputs Outputs Reference
@subsection Declaring I/O

Required attributes: @code{name}, @code{unit}, @code{min}, @code{max},
@code{step}, @code{val}. Optional: @code{description}, @code{flags}
(comma-separated, e.g.@: @code{no-history}).

Recognised units include: @code{null}, @code{celsius}, @code{percent},
@code{bar}, @code{onoff}, @code{enum}, @code{v}, @code{w}, @code{var},
@code{kw}, @code{wh}, @code{kwh}, @code{seconds}, @code{minutes},
@code{hours}.

On @code{<output>}, optional @code{<override-timeout>}: bare milliseconds,
or @code{60s} / @code{5m} / @code{1h} / @code{1000ms}. @code{0} or absent
= never auto-clear (@pxref{Command and Feedback}).

@node Bindings Reference
@subsection Bindings

@example
<bindings>
  <bind datapoint="temp-rounded" input="EX:temp"/>
  <bind datapoint="out-setpoint" output="EX:setpoint"/>
  <bind datapoint="out-mode-fb" output="EX:mode"
        role="feedback" settle-delay="5s"/>
</bindings>
@end example

@table @code
@item input=
Datapoint → @acronym{ST} input.
@item output=
Default @code{role} is command: output changes push the datapoint toward
the writer.
@item role="feedback"
Reader-driven datapoint mirrors the output under read-sync.
@item settle-delay
On feedback binds: ignore mismatches for this duration after a command
write (default @code{5s}; @code{0} disables). Same duration syntax as
@code{override-timeout}.
@end table

@node Writing Plugins
@chapter Writing Plugins

This chapter is for developers extending @acronym{STCS} with a new
@code{.so}.

@node Plugin Lifecycle
@section Plugin Lifecycle

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Scan} (runlevel 0). The service opens every shared module under
the plugin directory (files whose name ends with the platform module
suffix, e.g.@: @code{.so}). Each module must export
@code{stcs_plugin_get_info}. The returned @code{STCSPluginInfo} supplies
a unique @emph{name}, a @emph{category}, and a @emph{version}. The module
is then closed; only the metadata and path are kept.
@item
@strong{Instantiate} (config parse). When @acronym{XML} references
@code{plugin="that-name"}, the service reloads the module, resolves
@code{stcs_plugin_create}, and expects a @code{GObject} of the right
base type (@code{STCSTransport}, @code{STCSReader}, @code{STCSWriter}, or
@code{STCSTransform}).
@item
@strong{Configure}. The core calls the class @code{configure} vfunc with
the plugin's @acronym{XML} subtree (and, for readers/writers, a datapoint
lookup callback).
@end enumerate

Duplicate plugin names abort the scan. A missing
@code{stcs_plugin_get_info} symbol is a hard error for that file.

@node Plugin Categories and Exports
@section Categories and Required Exports

@example
#include <gmodule.h>
#include "plugin.h"
#include "starter/configurable-service/transform.h" /* or transport/reader/writer */

G_MODULE_EXPORT STCSPluginInfo *
stcs_plugin_get_info(void)
@{
  return stcs_plugin_info_new("my-transform",
                              STCS_PLUGIN_CATEGORY_TRANSFORM,
                              "1.0.0");
@}

G_MODULE_EXPORT GObject *
stcs_plugin_create(void)
@{
  return g_object_new(STCS_TYPE_MY_TRANSFORM, NULL);
@}
@end example

Categories (@code{STCSPluginCategory}):

@table @code
@item STCS_PLUGIN_CATEGORY_TRANSPORT
Subclass @code{STCSTransport}.
@item STCS_PLUGIN_CATEGORY_READER
Subclass @code{STCSReader}.
@item STCS_PLUGIN_CATEGORY_WRITER
Subclass @code{STCSWriter}.
@item STCS_PLUGIN_CATEGORY_TRANSFORM
Subclass @code{STCSTransform}.
@end table

The config @code{plugin="my-transform"} string must match the name passed
to @code{stcs_plugin_info_new}.

@node Building a Plugin
@section Building and Installing

Ship the module as a libtool @code{-module} shared object linked against
@code{libstarter-configurable-service} (and @code{starter-core} /
GLib as needed). Install it next to the other plugins so the scan finds
it, or point @option{--plugin-dir} at your build directory while
developing.

Headers of interest:

@itemize
@item @file{starter/configurable-service/plugin.h}
@item @file{starter/configurable-service/transport.h}
@item @file{starter/configurable-service/reader.h}
@item @file{starter/configurable-service/writer.h}
@item @file{starter/configurable-service/transform.h}
@item @file{starter/configurable-service/datapoint.h}
@end itemize

Study the shipped plugins under @file{plugins/} as templates.

@node Transport Plugin API
@section Transport Plugin API

Override on @code{STCSTransportClass}:

@table @code
@item configure
Parse the transport's @acronym{XML} children; return @code{FALSE} on error.
@item start_write
Begin one write. The base class guarantees at most one in flight. When
done, call @code{stcs_transport_finish_write()} or
@code{stcs_transport_finish_write_error()} with the same @code{op_id}.
The @code{GBytes} remains valid until finish.
@end table

For reads, emit @code{stcs_transport_emit_data_ready()} with the payload,
or @code{stcs_transport_emit_data_error()} on failure. Writers enqueue
via @code{stcs_transport_write()} (queue depth 1).

@node Reader Plugin API
@section Reader Plugin API

Override on @code{STCSReaderClass}:

@table @code
@item configure
Parse maps and options. Resolve datapoint ids with the provided lookup
callback, then @code{stcs_reader_add_mapping(key, datapoint)}.
@item parse
Given a @code{GBytes} payload, extract values and call
@code{stcs_reader_push_value(key, value)} for each mapped key.
@end table

The core binds the reader to its transport
(@code{stcs_reader_set_transport}) and connects @code{data-ready} /
@code{data-error}. Optional sync hooks wrap @code{parse} for feedback
alignment.

@node Writer Plugin API
@section Writer Plugin API

Override on @code{STCSWriterClass}:

@table @code
@item configure
Parse template and maps; @code{stcs_writer_add_mapping(key, datapoint)}.
@item write_value
Called when a mapped datapoint changes. Update the outbound buffer and
send it (typically @code{stcs_writer_write_bytes()}).
@end table

@code{<write-attempts>} is handled by the base writer (retry same bytes
on transport write-error). Connect acknowledgement via the writer ack
hook used by runlevel wiring.

@node Transform Plugin API
@section Transform Plugin API

Override on @code{STCSTransformClass}:

@table @code
@item configure
Parse transform-specific @acronym{XML}.
@item process
Map @code{in} → @code{*out}; return @code{FALSE} on error.
@end table

Transforms are owned by datapoints and run in order on every push.

@node Examples Directory
@chapter Examples Directory

The @file{examples/} tree is not installed as a system service pack.
The samples below are meant to be read alongside
@ref{Thinking in Pipelines}: both are pure @emph{sensor} services---data
enter STARTER; nothing is commanded.

@section SpaceTrack (@file{examples/space-track/space-track.stcs.xml})

@subheading What it does

@command{ST:SpaceTrack} polls a public @acronym{HTTP} @acronym{API}
(@uref{https://api.wheretheiss.at/v1/satellites/25544}) for the
International Space Station (NORAD id 25544) and publishes three
starter-core inputs:

@itemize
@item @code{SPTR:latitude}
@item @code{SPTR:longitude}
@item @code{SPTR:altitude} (kilometres)
@end itemize

There is no @code{<writers>} section and no @code{<outputs>}: the
service only feeds the core. Any rule such as ``notify when the ISS is
overhead'' would live in @command{starter-core}, not in this file.

@subheading Pipeline sketch

@example
every 15 minutes:
  GET wheretheiss.at JSON
    → json-reader
    → datapoints (round to 0.0001)
    → SPTR:latitude / longitude / altitude
@end example

@subheading Block by block

@table @asis
@item @code{<global>}
Registers the service as @code{ST:SpaceTrack} version @code{0.1.0} with
starter-core / mDNS at runlevel 3.

@item @code{<transports>} --- @code{iss-http}
@code{http-transport} in read mode: @code{GET} the satellite URL,
@code{Accept: application/json}, @code{auth type="none"}.
@code{<poll-cron>0 0 */15 * * *</poll-cron>} fires every fifteen minutes
on the clock (millisecond and second fields zero).

@item @code{<datapoints>}
Three ids (@code{latitude}, @code{longitude}, @code{altitude}). Each has
a @code{formula-transform} that rounds to four decimal places
(@code{floor(# * 10000 + 0.5) / 10000}) so tiny float jitter does not
dominate history or rule chatter.

@item @code{<readers>} --- @code{iss_json}
@code{json-reader} bound to @code{iss-http}. Three maps copy
@code{$.latitude}, @code{$.longitude}, and @code{$.altitude} into the
matching datapoints. Values are already numeric in JSON, so no
@code{<text-to-value>} is required.

@item @code{<inputs>}
Declares the three @acronym{ST} inputs with ranges suitable for Earth
coordinates and LEO altitude. @code{unit="null"} (plain numbers);
@code{step="0.0001"} matches the transform.

@item @code{<bindings>}
Links each datapoint to its input. That closes the read pipeline: after
a successful poll, starter-core peers see updated @code{SPTR:*} values.
@end table

@subheading How to run it

@example
$ starter-configurable-service \
    --config examples/space-track/space-track.stcs.xml
@end example

Requires network access to the @acronym{API}. Adjust @code{<poll-cron>}
if you need a different refresh rate (respect the remote service's usage
expectations).

@section Shelly H&T (@file{examples/shelly-ht/shelly-ht.stcs.xml})

@subheading What it does

@command{ST:Shelly-HT} polls a Shelly Humidity & Temperature device on
the local network (Gen1-style @code{/status} JSON) and publishes two
starter-core inputs:

@itemize
@item @code{ShellyHT:temperature} (@code{celsius})
@item @code{ShellyHT:humidity} (@code{percent})
@end itemize

Again there is no writer and no output: the device is treated only as a
pair of sensors. Climate or alert rules belong in @command{starter-core}.

@subheading Pipeline sketch

@example
every 15 seconds:
  GET http://<device>/status
    → json-reader ($.tmp.value / $.hum.value)
    → datapoints temperature / humidity
    → ShellyHT:temperature / ShellyHT:humidity
@end example

@subheading Block by block

@table @asis
@item @code{<global>}
Registers @code{ST:Shelly-HT} version @code{0.1.0}.

@item @code{<transports>} --- @code{status-http}
@code{http-transport} @code{GET} to the device @code{/status} URL
(placeholder @code{192.168.1.100}---change it to your H&T address),
@code{Accept: application/json}, no auth.
@code{<poll-cron>0 */15 * * * *</poll-cron>} runs every fifteen seconds.

@item @code{<datapoints>}
@code{temperature} and @code{humidity} with no transforms (the device
already reports usable floats).

@item @code{<readers>} --- @code{status-json}
@code{json-reader} on @code{status-http}. Gen1 Shelly H&T nests the
values under @code{$.tmp.value} and @code{$.hum.value}.

@item @code{<inputs>}
@code{ShellyHT:temperature} (@math{-10}..@math{50}@math{^\circ}C) and
@code{ShellyHT:humidity} (0..100 %).

@item @code{<bindings>}
Each datapoint to its input---read pipeline complete.
@end table

@subheading How to run it

@enumerate
@item
Edit @code{<url>} in the config so the host matches your Shelly H&T.
@item
Run:
@end enumerate

@example
$ starter-configurable-service \
    --config examples/shelly-ht/shelly-ht.stcs.xml
@end example

If your firmware exposes a different JSON layout (e.g.@: Gen2
@code{Shelly.GetStatus}), adjust the @code{path=} attributes on the
reader maps accordingly.

@section Current Time (@file{examples/current-time/})

@subheading What it does

@command{ST:Time} runs a tiny helper binary (@command{current-time}) via
@code{process-transport}. The helper prints one JSON object with local
wall-clock fields and exits. @acronym{STCS} maps selected fields to
starter-core inputs:

@itemize
@item @code{TIME:year}, @code{TIME:month}, @code{TIME:day}
@item @code{TIME:hour}, @code{TIME:minute}
@item @code{TIME:minuteOfDay} (minutes since local midnight)
@end itemize

This is the ``time as an input'' pattern from @ref{Thinking in
Pipelines}: rules in @command{starter-core} can schedule on the clock;
@acronym{STCS} only refreshes the numbers.

@subheading Helper binary

@file{examples/current-time/current-time.c} is built and installed as
@code{$(bindir)/current-time}. Sample output:

@example
@{"year": 2026, "month": 10, "day": 3, "hour": 8, "minute": 15,
 "second": 0, "dayMinutes": 495, "daySeconds": 29700,
 "unix": 1759473300, "UTCOffset": 7200000000, "timezone": "CEST"@}
@end example

(Exact @code{UTCOffset} / @code{timezone} depend on the host.)

@subheading Pipeline sketch

@example
every minute:
  exec current-time → stdout JSON
    → json-reader
    → datapoints
    → TIME:* inputs
@end example

@subheading Block by block

@table @asis
@item @code{<global>}
Registers @code{ST:Time} version @code{0.1.0}.

@item @code{<transports>} --- @code{current-time-process}
@code{process-transport} in @code{read} mode. @code{<command>} points at
the installed helper (@code{@@bindir@@/current-time} in the
@file{.xml.in} template, substituted at build time).
@code{<user>starter</user>} (change if that account is missing),
@code{<timeout-ms>1000</timeout-ms>},
@code{<poll-cron>0 1 * * * * *</poll-cron>} (every minute at second 1).

@item @code{<datapoints>}
Six ids matching the JSON keys we care about (@code{minuteOfDay} is fed
from @code{dayMinutes}).

@item @code{<readers>} --- @code{current-time-json}
@code{json-reader} maps @code{$.year} @dots{} @code{$.minute} and
@code{$.dayMinutes} → @code{minuteOfDay}. Unused helper fields are
ignored.

@item @code{<inputs>}
@code{TIME:*} with @code{flags="no-history"} so minute ticks do not fill
the core history database.

@item @code{<bindings>}
Each datapoint to its input.
@end table

@subheading How to run it

@example
$ ./configure && make && sudo make install
$ starter-configurable-service \
    --config $(prefix)/share/starter-configurable-service/examples/current-time/current-time.stcs.xml
@end example

Or point @option{--config} at the generated
@file{examples/current-time/current-time.stcs.xml} in the build tree after
@command{make}, and ensure @code{<command>} matches where the binary
was installed (or run from a tree where @code{bindir} was set
accordingly). The process-transport @code{<user>} must exist on the host
and the service must be allowed to @code{setuid} to it.

